<h1>recursive</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
(1) A data structure that is partially composed of other instances of the data structure.  For instance, a <a href="tree.html"><em>tree</em></a> is composed of smaller trees (<a href="subtree.html"><em>subtrees</em></a>) and <a href="leaf.html"><em>leaf</em></a> <a href="node.html"><em>nodes</em></a>, and a <a href="list.html"><em>list</em></a> may  have other lists as elements.  (2) An <a href="algorithm.html"><em>algorithm</em></a> in which functions might call themselves.  For instance, <a href="quicksort.html"><em>quicksort</em></a> or <a href="heapify.html"><em>heapify</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="recursion.html"><em>recursion</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Infinite data structures may be represented by having a tree include (point back to) itself recursively, a list include itself, etc.  Recursive data structures are often best handled with a recursive algorithm, or an algorithm using <a href="recursion.html"><em>recursion</em></a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
